WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.350
- Why couldn't we get enough masks?

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.850
Why couldn't we get enough ventilators?

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.570
Why weren't there the perception

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.510
that there weren't enough hospital beds?

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.480
How come the morgues couldn't keep up?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.035
And so on and so on.

00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:13.228
How come we couldn't be prepared for this?

00:00:13.228 --> 00:00:16.600
And no matter how you
look at it, at every area,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.208
there was a failure of government.

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:21.791
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.330
- I'm Dave Rubin, and this is

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.060
The Rubin Report reminder everybody,

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.450
lockdown is more fun with friends

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.797
and ad-free video and podcasts,

00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:37.920
so please consider joining
us at rubinreport.com

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.027
and joining me today is
the editorial director

00:00:41.027 --> 00:00:44.540
at the American Institute
for Economic Research,

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.063
Jeffrey Tucker, welcome
back to The Rubin Report.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.810
- It's good to be here, Dave.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:50.910
Nice to see you again.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.850
- Good to see you again.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.110
I'm sorry we're not doing this in person,

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.300
but we are social distancing
like everybody else,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.150
so, I guess my first question to you is,

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.130
well, just how ya doing?

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.933
How ya doing in a time of
social distancing and lockdown?

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.519
- (laughing) Well, I got to give

00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:11.670
the same answer anybody would,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.060
which is I can't believe that
this is the world we live in.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.200
I never would have imagined
this even three weeks ago

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.670
but somehow we're fairing.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.650
We're not starving, we're not dying,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.580
we're talking to people, and you know,

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.510
I wrote a book called,
"The Market Loves You,"

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.413
and all I can say is the market,

00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:34.520
although bludgeoned and truncated

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.252
and it's never been
attacked like this before

00:01:37.252 --> 00:01:39.061
maybe in the history of the world.

00:01:39.061 --> 00:01:41.170
It's still somehow working,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.363
so I'm very, very grateful for that.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.830
- Yeah, that seems like
the right start to this

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.430
because throughout the last month or so,

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.340
you're one of the few people I would say

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:54.980
that is making some
consistent sense to me,

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.380
at least on Twitter.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.240
I think you're a pretty great
classical liberal thinker,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.272
which is why I wanted to have you here

00:02:00.272 --> 00:02:01.670
because I want to talk about stimulus

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.790
and I want to talk about markets

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.350
and I want to talk about the
Constitution and free speech

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.780
and right to assembly
and all of those things

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.140
that I know you and I
and my audience all love,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.590
so, why don't we start with this,

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.810
which is just going off
what you just said there.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:21.180
We seem to be more resilient
perhaps than we thought.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.060
I mean, I think if you
would had said to people

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.587
six months ago, "hey, do you
know that in March and April,

00:02:25.587 --> 00:02:27.529
"you're gonna be shut
down, locked in your house

00:02:27.529 --> 00:02:29.867
"along with virtually
the rest of the world

00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:31.907
"for now going on five weeks,

00:02:31.907 --> 00:02:34.030
"and it could be months after that."

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.157
I think people would've thought,

00:02:35.157 --> 00:02:36.697
"oh, there's gonna be violence,

00:02:36.697 --> 00:02:37.887
"oh, there's gonna be looting,

00:02:37.887 --> 00:02:40.819
"there's gonna be crime,"
all of these things,

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:44.320
and yet as you started
us with, we're doing okay

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.430
and the markets actually
haven't completely tanked,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.593
we're going up and down, but
everything is kinda working.

00:02:51.618 --> 00:02:54.340
- Oh Dave, I could tell
you such inspiring stories.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.860
I just want to cry when I think about it.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.480
You know, when I go to the grocery store,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.980
which is one of the things you can do now.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.390
It's access to real life and I go in there

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.430
and I first of all make sure to thank

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.000
every single worker
there for all of their,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.800
that they're doing but I
like to talk to the managers

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.280
about how they decide what
they're going to do that day.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.830
You know, was there a
run on chicken yesterday?

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.370
What'd you do about that?

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.690
Was there a run on sausage?

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:21.698
What'd you do about the salads

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:24.750
over here in the deli section?

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.000
How are you getting toilet paper?

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.030
How are you rationing it

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.253
given that there's restrictions
on price movements?

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.840
How has this changed your lives?

00:03:35.696 --> 00:03:40.561
And all I hear are stories
of heroism and brilliance

00:03:40.561 --> 00:03:43.610
and it's not just individual brilliance,

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.090
it's institutional brilliance

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.680
and it's the brilliance
of the market economy.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.140
It's still working despite everything,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.853
despite being clobbered in
ways that were unimaginable

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.383
just a few months ago,
and if I can say so, Dave,

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.090
this brings to mind a real paradox,

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.820
the times we're living through right now.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:09.820
This shut down has presumed
the adaptability of society.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.277
The capacity of society
to make this radical turn,

00:04:14.277 --> 00:04:19.277
to go from one life to the
other life practically overnight

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:25.080
to adopt a seemingly impossible pose

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.590
and yet the government
edicts that have come down

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.270
have presumed the impossibility of society

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.910
to adapt to the presence of
pandemic disease, you know?

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.100
Like we can adapt to a complete--

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.890
- Is that adaptability?

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.310
Do you think that adaptability
is unique to capitalism

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.780
because of the way it
allows so many people

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.044
to be in the market and innovate and move

00:04:48.044 --> 00:04:50.693
as things need to be moved?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.750
- Yeah, it's embedded in the
essence of a free society

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.950
where people learn to be intelligent

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.948
and learn to control their world,

00:05:00.948 --> 00:05:02.294
learn to cooperate with people

00:05:02.294 --> 00:05:06.030
and create these market
signals that tell us in prices,

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.913
and interest, and all these things.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.180
I think at the end of this,

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.450
if you're not a libertarian
you're not paying attention

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:20.450
because the market has
worked and brilliantly

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.508
and it's only not worked
when it's been hobbled,

00:05:23.508 --> 00:05:25.710
and it has been hobbled.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.710
It was hobbled before
this crisis actually.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.410
We can talk about all the specific ways

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.243
in which that's true.

00:05:31.243 --> 00:05:36.160
I blame, I don't, again,
I don't blame coronavirus

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.350
for your suffering right now.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.150
Viruses have always been
with us and always will be,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.137
and society had developed
ways to deal with this virus,

00:05:43.137 --> 00:05:45.920
and medical professionals especially

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.310
have learned how to deal with a virus,

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.850
and it's a challenge for any
society under any conditions

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.280
but what you want above all else

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.510
is freedom and adaptability,

00:05:56.510 --> 00:06:00.240
and to decentralize decision-making

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.750
and then you ask, Dave, for some reason,

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.330
we took a completely different direction.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.490
Fortunately we haven't
shut down the stock market,

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.530
we haven't shut down essential stores

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.810
and it's thanks to that
we're eating today,

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.240
that we're sleeping today,
that we're able to get by,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.810
so it's not the government that we owe

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.470
the fact that we're
surviving through this.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:25.623
It's 100% the element of freedom
that remains in the system.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.210
- But Jeffrey, I keep
reading all these brilliant

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:33.100
think pieces saying how
this shows the failure

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.710
of libertarianism and limited government

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:38.370
because this, if ever, this is the moment

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.460
we need big government to do things

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.670
and the very people who have
been calling Trump Hitler

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.420
for four years, are now
demanding that he do more

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:50.160
with his power, am I not
understanding things properly?

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.996
What am I missing here?

00:06:51.996 --> 00:06:54.260
- (laughing) I tell you
what, I think you and I

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.460
share the same frustration.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.940
The response of the left
has been alarming to me

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.350
to the extent that I find indescribable

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:06.600
because I think last
time I was on your show,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.410
I myself was warning about the danger

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.873
of the authoritarianism of
Trump and we can get to that.

00:07:12.873 --> 00:07:13.706
- Yeah, yeah.
- I think he's made

00:07:13.706 --> 00:07:17.310
some bad decisions here and
they stem from his experiences--

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:18.804
- Let's explore all of it, for sure.

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:21.800
- Yeah and the extent
from his own nationalism

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.780
and his own illiberalism, really,

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.940
which I think it's seriously problematic

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:32.940
but my God, day after
day to see these howling,

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:38.090
oppressed people all have
decided that the narrative

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:42.060
is that Trump is some
sort of Bastiat figure

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.280
who's just not doing anything

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.993
and that people are dying because of him.

00:07:48.196 --> 00:07:52.330
It's a real shock and I don't
wanna say I'm grateful for him

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.440
because I think he's made his errors

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:59.300
but for day-to-day I'm
sure you feel the same way,

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.800
my God if the Trump
administration went along

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.450
with what CNN, MSNBC and
the, I'm sorry to say,

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.177
unspeakable "New York Times",

00:08:08.177 --> 00:08:11.970
"New York Times", a venue
that I've always adored

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:12.986
and always read and always loved.

00:08:12.986 --> 00:08:14.069
- Yeah, yeah.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:20.220
- And to see what they did,
what they helped enable,

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:24.400
I just don't know.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.523
If there's any such thing
as moral culpability,

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:31.740
I think, I'm so sorry to say
it, again it breaks my heart,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.810
I think the "New York
Times" is morally culpable

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.090
for what's happened to this country today

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.810
and I listen to "The Daily" podcast

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.510
and I saw the hysteria being ramped up

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.600
and the demands for crackdowns

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.450
and the call for a
medieval response to this

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.660
and the demands for, I don't
know what you would call it

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:53.370
but a fascist, communist
social distancing policies

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:57.680
and it's been grim and shocking
and you've been shocked,

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.020
I've been shocked, I
don't know what to say.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.250
- So it's so interesting
that you're as concerned

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:03.880
about the media element as I am

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:04.910
because it seems to me

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.010
that we're fighting two viruses right now.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.130
We all know about the
coronavirus and how to fight that

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:13.890
but the misinformation
virus that is now permeating

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.910
throughout the media and
it's "New York Times"

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.903
and it's Vox and it's CNN
and the list goes on and on.

00:09:19.903 --> 00:09:23.110
It's making it almost
impossible for any of us

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:25.720
to have what I would say
is a sane conversation

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:26.780
because we're all dealing

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.230
with different sets of information.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:32.440
How concerned generally
are you about that?

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.050
- Tremendously!

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.680
I actually, I summarized this,

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.990
you know we've all been talking to friends

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.240
and what's happened and things

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.550
but I memorized it the other day

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:46.550
that I really blame hucksters, scientists,

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.883
fake scientists comforted
by the howling media,

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:57.510
anti-Trump media combined with
a panicked political class

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.750
and it was a kind of a perfect storm.

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:04.350
Those three elements cam
together and lead to a level

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:09.330
of wreckage that's
unspeakable and unconventional

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.840
morally and I have to tell you

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.740
because for years people have told me

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.190
about the evil of the mainstream media

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:20.753
and I've never really wanted to go there.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:23.598
I'm a big believer in press freedom

00:10:23.598 --> 00:10:26.120
and I never wanted to
be one of those people

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:27.820
to trash "The New York Times"

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:30.811
and actually I love "The New
York Times", I always have

00:10:30.811 --> 00:10:34.700
but I feel like I've gotten
a real wake up call here,

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:35.580
a real shock.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:39.350
I don't know how you can be
in the mainstream press today

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:43.000
and be salivating over
the clicks and the views

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.800
and the amount of ad buys that
you're generating from this

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.653
while watching freedom
just go down the drain.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:56.610
It's been a shock to me, I
just can't even describe to you

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.750
how horrifying it's been to
me and to tell you the truth,

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.700
I've really turned it off, I've had to.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.890
- Yeah, I think, well we know
that people are turning it off

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:11.270
in tremendous numbers and
watching things like this instead,

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:14.590
so as a liberty-minded
person, as you said,

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:17.440
if people don't come out
of this more libertarian,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:19.030
something will have gone wrong,

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:22.530
what are the proper approaches

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:24.970
for a liberty-minded government?

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:29.970
So, say a libertarian or
classic liberal set of ideas

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.730
that could get us through
something like this,

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:35.920
what should we have done at the beginning,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.300
what should we have done as
we're going through it right now

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.940
and what should we be
looking at for the future

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:43.950
if you want to keep
people as free as possible

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.695
while also dealing with something

00:11:45.695 --> 00:11:47.593
that we've never dealt with before.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:52.470
- Dave, if I were gonna summarize
my position in a sentence,

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:54.890
it would be something like this,

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.590
the medical professionals
in the medical industry,

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.740
free of all restrictions

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.110
should have been allowed
to deal with this virus

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:05.360
from the very beginning

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.740
without the intervention
of politicians, period.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:09.870
I mean, that I think is the end.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.940
- So basically, just get rid of red tape,

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.280
just let them do their thing,

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.544
no more regulations on
experimental treatments,

00:12:15.544 --> 00:12:16.683
that kind of stuff?

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:21.720
- Yeah and not only that but
if you think about it, Dave,

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.460
a lot of this flatten the curve analysis

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.060
that lead to the crackdowns

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.010
and the mandatory social distancing

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:30.550
and the universal house imprisonment

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.910
that we're dealing with right now

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:35.880
was due to the perception
that the medical industry

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.690
was unscalable in the event of emergency.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.430
Now that's very interesting,

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:44.310
people need to be asked
the question, if it's true,

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.483
why was an industry unable to scale?

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.310
Industry is specialized in scaling,

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:54.560
when people buy more of your product,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.540
as an industry you're
excited to offer more

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.780
of that product or service to them,

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:02.640
what makes the medical industry
unique in that respect?

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.830
Why couldn't we get enough masks?

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.340
Why couldn't we get enough ventilators?

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.060
Why weren't there, the perception

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:10.000
that there weren't enough hospital beds?

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:11.970
How come the morgues couldn't keep up?

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:13.870
And so on and so on,

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.030
how come we couldn't be prepared for this?

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.910
And no matter how you look at it,

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:21.870
at every area was a failure of government.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:26.080
It was that the system
was heavily regulated,

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:29.170
the strategic medical reserve
disincentivized hospitals

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:33.380
from gathering their own supplies.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:37.120
The tariffs on masks
seriously inhibited them

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.190
and this is very personal to me

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.520
because I have friends in New York

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:43.580
who are nurses and doctors
who were sharing masks

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.080
and they can't get enough

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.030
and the tariffs seriously hindered that

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.400
and the hospitals didn't
have enough supplies on-hand,

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.010
'cause they assumed that it was all there,

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.040
that federal government
was putting it all together

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.600
but then of course they
opened the warehouse

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.030
and the half ventilators don't work.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.280
The supplies weren't there

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.678
'cause they'd previously been depleted.

00:14:02.678 --> 00:14:06.161
Every area, all you see is
government irrationality,

00:14:06.161 --> 00:14:08.480
the inability of hospitals to scale,

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:09.990
the panic of the political class

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.930
to force everybody into their homes,

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.740
which is definitely
where you should not be,

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:18.320
whether you have respiratory disease,

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:21.420
the answer is to get outside
as everybody understands.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.380
You don't wanna put old
people with young people

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:26.160
'cause that causes endless
amounts of infections.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.740
You actually wanna isolate
and separate the people

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.730
who are vulnerable to this disease,

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:33.800
which are the old and frail

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:38.275
and last thing you wanna
do is close the schools,

00:14:38.275 --> 00:14:43.275
'cause COVID, as we know
know, is actually not fatal

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:48.730
for young, healthy people, so
at every single area, Dave,

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.013
the government has done the wrong thing

00:14:51.013 --> 00:14:54.560
and I don't know how long or
how detailed we wanna get here

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.320
but the testing fiasco was
the great, I would say,

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.350
beginning of all of this stuff

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:03.650
because China had already gone through

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:05.150
a trail run of this stuff.

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.932
We had this benefit of
this, we saw this coming

00:15:08.932 --> 00:15:11.600
and the CDC and the FDA stopped,

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:13.050
and this is a matter of record,

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:17.710
really stopped private researchers
from developing the test

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:19.440
and distributing the test
from the very beginning

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.840
of this crisis, so that
was a catastrophic thing

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.950
and that lead to what
I've called, pardon me,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.530
an epistemic crises, we didn't know.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.330
We didn't know who had
it, whether I had it,

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:33.319
whether you had it, what
we should do about it

00:15:33.319 --> 00:15:34.616
and how it was transmitted,

00:15:34.616 --> 00:15:35.500
who was vulnerable to the fatalities,

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.690
what the case fatality rate
is, we didn't know anything!

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:42.280
So we replaced knowledge
that we could have used

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:47.280
with power and panic
and here we are today.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.160
- So is one of the inherent problems,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.570
that we have fighting the bad ideas

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:55.560
that would encroach on our rights,

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.450
that even if you lay out to people,

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.380
well here's all the things
that government has done wrong

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:03.991
all the way and here's why
it can never work properly,

00:16:03.991 --> 00:16:06.337
that they always say,
"well that just means

00:16:06.337 --> 00:16:10.087
"we need more government, that
just means we need to give it

00:16:10.087 --> 00:16:11.737
"more money so it can be bigger,

00:16:11.737 --> 00:16:13.857
"so that we can put more studies out there

00:16:13.857 --> 00:16:16.210
"to make sure it knows
how to do these things",

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.950
and that answer always sort
of sounds easy and right

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.980
for people that aren't really thinking.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:23.276
It's much harder to say to people,

00:16:23.276 --> 00:16:27.307
"oh, the government actually
should've been scaled

00:16:27.307 --> 00:16:30.787
"way back on this, so we could
have let the professionals

00:16:30.787 --> 00:16:31.620
"deal with it,"

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.510
that sorta sounds scary to people,

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:34.897
especially at a time
right now where we feel

00:16:34.897 --> 00:16:39.073
like the future is just
up in the air right now.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:44.820
- Yeah and I think this speaks
to a little bit of something,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.940
a principle that's animated
your career in your life,

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:51.510
which, you had a passion some years ago,

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:56.510
a realization that people
don't appreciated freedom.

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:00.410
They don't have confidence in freedom,

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:03.370
that freedom was being
attacked fundamentally

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:08.370
and philosophically by both
the left and on the right too

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:11.730
and I think you worried about that,

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:15.920
you actually asked yourself
a very important question,

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.860
what happens in the event of a crisis

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:21.260
where we need freedom more than ever?

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:24.240
And yet, we don't have the
intellectual foundations in place

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.730
where there's not a cultural love

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.130
of this thing we call freedom

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:31.540
or much less an understanding
of it, what happens then?

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:34.730
And I think that was a
very interesting perception

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:38.810
that you had several years ago,

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:40.940
that you really dedicated
yourself really hard

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:45.500
to this problem and tried your
best to do something about it

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.620
but I think what we're seeing right now

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:51.653
are the results of a
culture that's forgotten

00:17:51.653 --> 00:17:55.890
the meaning of freedom
and what if you lose that,

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.110
what do you replace it with?

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:01.262
You replace it with the police
state, with poor planning,

00:18:01.262 --> 00:18:02.823
with chaos, yeah.
- Yeah, something not good,

00:18:06.733 --> 00:18:11.233
so right now as we sit here
all trapped in our houses,

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.470
trying to figure out what to do,

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:18.380
how concerned are you about
some of those basic freedoms

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:19.213
that we're talking about?

00:18:19.213 --> 00:18:22.170
I mean, right now the right
to assembly, for example,

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:25.640
we cannot assemble together,
you can not get 10 people

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:26.690
in a room to do anything.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:30.605
We had our bizarre mayor, I'm being nice,

00:18:30.605 --> 00:18:34.210
de Blasio in New York City,
say that if he found temples

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:35.970
or churches that were
having any gatherings,

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:38.120
they could be permanently closed.

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:41.100
Now again, I'm all for the
six foot social distancing

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.890
if that's what the
professionals are telling us

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:45.290
but the idea that we could
permanently close these things

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:48.320
and we can't assemble or
our right to free speech

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.740
in that we know that even right now,

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.050
big tech is removing blog posts
that run counter knowledge.

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:57.740
That's not necessarily
the government stepping in

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.570
and doing it but if we look
at some of our key freedoms,

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.960
assembly and speech being
the two at the moment,

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:04.010
how concerned are you

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:06.770
that we're just gonna sort of
put them aside throughout this

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.377
and then it'll be six
months later and we'll go,

00:19:08.377 --> 00:19:10.027
"oh, we had those things?"

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:14.170
- Yeah, I think anything is
possible right now, Dave,

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:17.453
and anybody who says otherwise
hasn't been paying attention

00:19:17.453 --> 00:19:20.120
over the past couple of
weeks, there's no chance,

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:23.520
so I looked before we met up just now,

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.830
I looked back at what
I wrote January 27th,

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:31.070
'cause I wrote an article
warning that the quarantine power

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:32.920
could be used and explained

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:35.860
why this is a very, very bad idea

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:40.860
but even in that article, I
imagined something like this

00:19:41.632 --> 00:19:45.610
that the government would
set up quarantine areas,

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:47.340
which in fact they have them,

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:48.910
and they would come knocking on your door

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:51.300
and grab people out of it,
maybe one family at a time

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:54.190
and put them in the quarantine area

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:58.826
or they would target maybe neighborhoods

00:19:58.826 --> 00:20:02.080
and round everybody up
and put in paddy wagons

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.210
and put 'em in the quarantine area

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:07.470
but even January 27th, I never imagined

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:10.900
that governments of the
world would put everybody

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:13.450
under what amounts to house arrest,

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:17.280
so the face that this has happened,

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:19.840
I would say at this point,
anything's possible.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.540
I worried 'cause we put
out a book last week

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:24.904
called "Coronavirus and Economic Crisis",

00:20:24.904 --> 00:20:28.370
a book put out by American
Institute for Economic Research

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:30.620
and I put it on Amazon
and I gotta tell you,

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:34.160
I was worried, I was worried
that Amazon would take it down,

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:39.160
so I printed separately
lots of copies independently

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:42.290
with an independent publisher

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.350
and started making it
available on the website,

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:47.540
just in case we face massive censorship.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.390
I actually, if I were
going to describe it,

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:52.280
I would say we're that close, that close

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.830
and once you get rid of, and
it's very interesting to me

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:59.440
that you use this word right,

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:03.120
you've been using this
repeated a right to assemble,

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:06.030
a right to associate, a right to speak.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.890
A right is not something
that's contention,

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:11.430
it's something that you actually possess.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.150
It's not granted to you by the government,

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:15.990
it's something you own by
virtue of your humanity.

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:19.180
It's hard to talk about
rights in these times

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:22.867
and we don't, apparently, have
the right to our own property

00:21:22.867 --> 00:21:24.870
'cause the government can
shut down our business

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:27.370
on grounds that it's unessential, right?

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:32.370
We apparently don't have the
right even to leave our homes,

00:21:35.288 --> 00:21:39.310
so once you take that away, I mean Dave,

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:41.710
I don't wanna be an alarmist
about this or anything

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:43.880
but look, from a philosophical

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.330
or intellectual point of view,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:48.310
we are living the practical reality

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:50.201
of what comes very close to being

00:21:50.201 --> 00:21:53.263
a totalitarian state right now.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:58.960
Now fortunately, thank
you political leaders,

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:00.760
you're letting the grocery stores work,

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.363
fortunately they're private.

00:22:04.513 --> 00:22:08.620
Thank you for letting us have pharmacies,

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:10.113
they're private entities too,

00:22:11.169 --> 00:22:15.610
so we're grateful for the
little bit of private markets

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:19.530
that are left but the
attack on everybody else

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:21.420
and the attack on our rights is shocking

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:26.053
and the social consequences of
this are unspeakable, really.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.900
People are not gonna comply,
they're not complying now.

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:31.980
I'm sure you know this,

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:33.280
in fact you've probably attended them

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:34.840
but there are speakeasies

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.900
and all over New York City, popping up

00:22:38.104 --> 00:22:41.020
where you can go to dinner
and gather in more than five,

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:44.520
more than 10, even 30
people and they're drinking,

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:45.520
they're having dinner together,

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:47.763
they are in defiance of the law.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:51.130
Friends of mine in Manhattan
sit in their penthouses

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:56.130
and every night after
nightfall people rush out

00:22:56.513 --> 00:22:59.200
into the streets with noise makers

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:02.150
just to illustrate that
they believe in freedom

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:07.243
and for God's sake, do we not
believe in freedom anymore?

00:23:08.450 --> 00:23:09.522
The politicians presume

00:23:09.522 --> 00:23:12.770
that we're like pieces on a chess board

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:14.260
that can just move around

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:15.093
but you know what?

00:23:15.093 --> 00:23:17.620
That's not true, that
is not the human mind.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.730
We are not inanimate objects,
we are not birds in cages,

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.071
we will live free, one way or the other.

00:23:22.071 --> 00:23:23.890
- But so what would you say?

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:25.760
So of course I'm with you
but what would you say

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:26.677
to the people who would say,

00:23:26.677 --> 00:23:29.647
"but all of those people, your
friends that are going out

00:23:29.647 --> 00:23:31.767
"onto the streets at night and
the people that are getting

00:23:31.767 --> 00:23:34.607
"together at speakeasies and going out

00:23:34.607 --> 00:23:36.357
"more than they have to
go out and the rest of it

00:23:36.357 --> 00:23:38.577
"and exercising their rights and freedom,

00:23:38.577 --> 00:23:40.987
"that those are the people
that are gonna keep this thing

00:23:40.987 --> 00:23:44.197
"spreading because people
don't know when to rein in

00:23:44.197 --> 00:23:46.137
"their own personal responsibility

00:23:46.137 --> 00:23:50.170
"and do something so that
society doesn't crumble",

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:51.003
something like that.

00:23:51.003 --> 00:23:55.690
- Well at the height end
of the enlightenment,

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:58.000
where we discovered this
idea of human freedom,

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.750
there was also disease (chuckling).

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:04.160
We didn't have very clean water,

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:09.160
there were annual viruses that
came along and killed people

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:11.530
and there were pandemics and so on

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:15.370
but they still, somehow, came to believe

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:17.180
that human beings have the right to speak

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:19.790
and associate and trade
and we work around it

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:22.190
and part of the belief in freedom,

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.780
is the confidence that
society has the intelligence

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:28.260
to deal with things like
viruses that come along

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:31.683
and the existence of
disease does not disable

00:24:31.683 --> 00:24:35.270
the whole of modernity, it just doesn't,

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:36.980
we can't think this way!

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:40.914
Modernity with forged at a
time when there were diseases

00:24:40.914 --> 00:24:45.914
and even back to the Black Death in Venice

00:24:46.382 --> 00:24:50.240
in the 13th century when there
was still very dangerous,

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:52.382
they still kept trade going.

00:24:52.382 --> 00:24:55.650
There was some towns would
come up with the restrictions

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:57.214
and that sort of thing

00:24:57.214 --> 00:25:01.170
but the idea of violating people's rights,

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:04.630
fundamental rights to
live and choose and speak

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:08.300
and everything was even
unthinkable 500 years ago.

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:10.430
The odd response we've done here

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:13.570
is premodern, it's medieval,

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:16.960
it's 11th and 12th century style stuff

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:20.410
except with 21st century technology

00:25:21.353 --> 00:25:24.629
and we're not responding to
these things scientifically

00:25:24.629 --> 00:25:27.930
even the slightest bit.

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:29.304
The response by the political class

00:25:29.304 --> 00:25:33.760
and I mean from about March 15th onwards,

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:38.420
was nothing short of
mystical, superstitious,

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:39.860
it was a panicked response.

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:44.550
Let's just throw everybody in
a cage of their own creation

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:47.200
and punish anybody for coming out of it

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.400
and I totally forgive people

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.090
who are going to these speakeasies,

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.646
you could say that they're prudent,

00:25:53.646 --> 00:25:56.360
they shouldn't be doing
that but for God's sake,

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.140
people exercising that fundamental right,

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:01.680
I'd say we need more of that
not less of that right now.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.140
- Do you think that's
partly just human nature

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:07.230
that so many people just
operate purely in fear,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:10.270
so a month ago when we
had that two day window

00:26:10.270 --> 00:26:12.717
where suddenly everything
shut down out of nowhere.

00:26:12.717 --> 00:26:15.730
They announced the entire
NBA season was canceled

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.160
and then they canceled
all these public events

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:21.460
and everything sort of
happened within a two day thing

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.139
that there becomes this odd rush to cancel

00:26:24.139 --> 00:26:27.050
because you don't wanna be the one guy

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:29.070
who's going against the crowd, right?

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:30.667
You don't wanna be the one guys who says,

00:26:30.667 --> 00:26:32.457
"no, we're not gonna cancel our season,

00:26:32.457 --> 00:26:34.477
"we're not gonna cancel all of our things,

00:26:34.477 --> 00:26:36.110
"we're gonna wait it out a little bit,"

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:39.190
knowing that the risk you
take of being just destroyed

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:41.620
by the media and everything
else on the other side,

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:45.450
is probably worse than just
going along with the crowd,

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:46.610
with the wave.

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:49.220
- Yeah, I understand your point.

00:26:49.220 --> 00:26:52.831
I have my doubts that there
was any public support for this

00:26:52.831 --> 00:26:54.200
actually at all.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.120
Now it's entirely up to private venues

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.920
whether or not they wanna
have these gathering

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:02.320
or accept the liability
associated with NBA games

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:05.040
or South by Southwest
and that sort of thing.

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:08.370
South by Southwest didn't
choose to cancel the conference,

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:09.610
that was a governmental decision,

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:13.370
so I'm looking at this and I'm
not saying whether that was,

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:15.750
maybe South by Southwest
should have canceled,

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:17.430
I don't know, I think about this a lot.

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:18.610
I'm not actually sure about that

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:20.070
'cause that's mostly
all young people there

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:23.280
who are actually not in any
great danger from COVID,

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.570
so I'm not sure about that

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:27.460
and it's hard to do factuals here

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:32.460
but with this much, Dave, I
know and I think you know too.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:39.073
Politicians, by which I
mean governors, mayors,

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:41.677
senators and congressmen and so on,

00:27:41.677 --> 00:27:44.440
are not owners of anything.

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:49.440
Their main job is to get
elected again, that's it,

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:53.970
so they don't really have a
financial, economic stake.

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.490
They don't have skin in the
game apart from the desire

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.340
to get voters to vote for them again

00:27:58.340 --> 00:28:00.130
and there was a sudden panic

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:02.980
on the part of political
leaders at all levels, really,

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:07.050
that if anybody died
while they were in office,

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.293
that they would somehow
be blamed for this,

00:28:10.293 --> 00:28:13.358
unless they just bludgeoned people

00:28:13.358 --> 00:28:16.260
and ruined people's lives,
so they somehow believe

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.130
there's less risk to
steal people's property

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:21.300
and arrest people in their homes

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:26.300
than it was to be blamed
for a public death

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:32.570
and that gets us back to a problem

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:35.660
that began this discussion,
the role of the media here.

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:38.270
'Cause what they're really
fearing, ultimately,

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:43.260
is this cancel culture, the Twitter mobs

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:46.140
and "The New York Times" editorial page

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.522
and the media culture

00:28:48.522 --> 00:28:52.503
that's lost all rationality and clarity.

00:28:53.629 --> 00:28:56.558
They're afraid of being denounced

00:28:56.558 --> 00:28:59.730
and dragged through the mud
and drowned and quartered

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.050
and so on by the press,

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:03.075
so I ultimately, it
really does come down--

00:29:03.075 --> 00:29:05.525
- Wouldn't that feel like
a perfect storm to you?

00:29:06.466 --> 00:29:07.299
- Yeah and--
- Doesn't that feel

00:29:07.299 --> 00:29:09.760
like a perfect storm
of the end of the media

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:11.940
that we've all been seeing
and as you said before,

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.940
you didn't even want to see it

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:15.580
but that so many of us have seen

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:18.970
the end of mainstream media,
we've watched our academic

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:20.790
and political institutions crumble

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:22.631
and then something like this comes in

00:29:22.631 --> 00:29:26.830
and just takes the last
remaining foundation of it

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:29.870
and then adds fear to it
and you take fearful people

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:32.130
who don't even understand
what their rights are

00:29:32.130 --> 00:29:33.443
as you alluded to earlier.

00:29:33.443 --> 00:29:34.443
- Right.

00:29:34.443 --> 00:29:36.232
- And then you really
have the perfect storm

00:29:36.232 --> 00:29:38.223
to control everybody.

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:42.055
- I like this phrase, the perfect storm,

00:29:42.055 --> 00:29:44.150
and I think it's the best
description to explain

00:29:44.150 --> 00:29:45.550
what's happened to us, really.

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:50.530
It wasn't really a conspiracy from the top

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:52.720
but of course, you know
there's conspiracy theories,

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:54.480
so we'll never stop them.

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:56.460
They've been generated by the day

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:59.337
and I totally forgive that,
people wanna understand

00:29:59.337 --> 00:30:00.760
what's happened to their freedoms,

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:02.040
what's happened to their rights,

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:04.180
why did the world go dark suddenly?

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:06.228
And people assume there
was some central plan

00:30:06.228 --> 00:30:07.770
but I don't believe that actually,

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:08.603
I think it was just this
combination of factors.

00:30:08.603 --> 00:30:11.571
- Yeah.
- And I've been wanting

00:30:11.571 --> 00:30:13.790
to write about this for a while but yeah,

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:17.070
it is a perfect storm
it was a howling media,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:21.734
panicked politicians
and just this onslaught,

00:30:21.734 --> 00:30:25.170
combined with a lack of information,

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.200
what I've described as an academic crisis

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:29.230
that led to a complete panic

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:31.680
but we're gonna be
spending the next 10 years

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.853
trying to figure this out,
exactly how did this happen?

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:38.270
Let me just draw attention
to one thing Dave,

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:39.977
and I'm not sure you're
really with me on this

00:30:39.977 --> 00:30:44.194
"New York Times" that I'm a
part of, probably you're not,

00:30:44.194 --> 00:30:46.714
you left it long ago but
I have been listening

00:30:46.714 --> 00:30:49.270
to "New York Times'" "The
Daily" for a long time

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.020
and I'm an admirer, I'm
friends with Michael Barbaro

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:55.990
and I like, I think these
reporters are fantastic

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:58.935
and I want this to be a good paper

00:30:58.935 --> 00:30:59.890
but I'm not sure whether once I've--

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:01.573
- Of course, I want it to be too.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:03.533
- Yeah, yeah, let's--
- Yeah, well let me

00:31:03.533 --> 00:31:05.560
just be really clear on
that, I want it to be.

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:07.590
Every time, even in the last few days

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:10.210
where I've had to go after
"New York Times" people,

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:11.470
just in the last few days,

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:14.580
they did a piece on misinformation
in the age of corona

00:31:14.580 --> 00:31:16.370
and they included my name in a chart

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:18.170
about a tweet that went viral

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.670
and then I tweeted about
it, I tweeted about it

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:23.280
and I didn't even tag the author,

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:24.470
I had never even heard of him before

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:26.218
but then he tweets back at me

00:31:26.218 --> 00:31:29.470
and says that I wasn't even
mentioned in the article,

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:31.570
even though my name was in the chart

00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:32.570
in the middle of the article,

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:34.710
he said that doesn't count as a mention

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:37.390
and it's like, you guys
with all your double speak

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:41.013
and nonsense, my name is in
the chart in the article.

00:31:41.998 --> 00:31:45.620
I want them to be good, I
don't like it when people

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:47.267
come up to me on the street and say,

00:31:47.267 --> 00:31:49.440
"Dave, you're one of
the few people I trust,"

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:51.268
I go, "oh man, we're in a lot of trouble,"

00:31:51.268 --> 00:31:52.440
you know what I mean?

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:54.600
They just have to be
a little less horrible

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:56.410
but they seemingly can't do it,

00:31:56.410 --> 00:31:58.720
so I'm with you on that,
although maybe I never thought

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:00.420
they were as wonderful as you did.

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.730
- It's been heartbreaking
for me because I think,

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:08.070
and I have to look back at the days here,

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:09.410
'cause everything's a fog, you know?

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:11.690
It's a fog of corona as they say

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:15.040
but I think it might have been early March

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:16.860
when "The New York Times"
"The Daily" podcast,

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:19.370
which is listened to by
millions and millions of people.

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:22.240
I mean, it's beautiful podcast,

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:25.130
beautifully produced, fantastic venue.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:29.670
They have on this reporter,
a disease reporter

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:34.670
and he comes on the air and
begins to raise public panic.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:39.418
Now there was no panic in those days

00:32:39.418 --> 00:32:41.074
and you'd think "The
New York Times" would be

00:32:41.074 --> 00:32:44.190
like a responsible, and usually
they're trying to diminish

00:32:44.190 --> 00:32:46.570
the panic, they're trying
to calm everybody down,

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:48.420
that's usually the ethos.

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:49.957
This was the opposite, he was going,

00:32:49.957 --> 00:32:51.677
"we're all going to die, oh my God,

00:32:51.677 --> 00:32:54.477
"we better carry Purell
with you everywhere

00:32:54.477 --> 00:32:57.222
"and spray every single
surface with hydrogen peroxide

00:32:57.222 --> 00:32:59.277
"and if we wanna get rid of this,

00:32:59.277 --> 00:33:01.487
"we're all going to have to drop in place,

00:33:01.487 --> 00:33:05.657
"wherever you happen to be
and live there for two months

00:33:05.657 --> 00:33:08.340
"or else this is gonna wipe
out the whole of humanity",

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:09.830
this is a guy who was saying this stuff

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:12.130
and he has this authoritative voice

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:14.370
and he's very compelling and everything.

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:16.740
I listened about the third
podcast with this guy,

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:20.880
now you get three times to
take over a what so amounts

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:24.930
to a 25 minute show,
three times in two weeks,

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:28.133
you better be good but I
looked up the guy's background.

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:30.840
Is he an epidemiologist?

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:32.210
No, was he trained in medicine?

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:34.160
No, does he know anything about viruses?

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:39.160
No, he, Dave, this gentleman
has a degree in rhetoric

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:43.155
from Berkeley University.

00:33:43.155 --> 00:33:44.650
A degree in--
- I was gonna

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:47.223
guess creative writing,
rhetoric is even better.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.410
- Yeah, so--
- Yeah.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:55.410
- And he's 66-years-old,
so I'm thinking about this,

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:57.250
what is his expertise?

00:33:57.250 --> 00:34:00.090
He's got a great voice for radio

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:02.410
and he's some sort of totalitarian,

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:05.510
I don't know what else to say
but they gave this guy this,

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.040
basically what amounts
to an hour and a half

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:09.600
over the course of two weeks

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:14.590
to spout of totalitarian blather and BS

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:16.480
to their audience of millions--

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:20.240
- Well how much of that do you think

00:34:20.240 --> 00:34:23.000
is just that "The New
York Times" in this case,

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:24.620
'cause that's who we
happen to be talking about

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:28.320
but we can talk about it
within a broader media lens,

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.880
is that they've invested so much time

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:33.850
in a Russia collusion hoax,
they've invested so much time

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:37.710
in impeachment and all of
these things that did not work

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:42.410
that in many ways, as Bill
Maher said about a year ago,

00:34:42.410 --> 00:34:44.870
he would be all for the economy tanking

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:47.120
if that's what it takes to take out Trump,

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:50.820
that they now see a little
blood in the water here

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:54.327
and they go, "oh, if we have
to destroy the entire country

00:34:54.327 --> 00:34:57.207
"to make sure this guy
doesn't win in November,

00:34:57.207 --> 00:34:59.860
"we'll kinda do it because
we got nothing left."

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:01.844
I mean, this is the one,

00:35:01.844 --> 00:35:03.760
it's one we couldn't have thought of

00:35:03.760 --> 00:35:05.610
and it's the one that they have left.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:11.940
- Well I'm stunned that
you brought up this idea

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:13.270
and I have to admit

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:16.743
that it hasn't been
entirely out of my mind,

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:19.803
I have actually considered this too.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:22.313
It's such a perverse
thought, I think we'd need

00:35:22.313 --> 00:35:23.795
Sigmund Freud himself to
take care of this one.

00:35:23.795 --> 00:35:24.962
- [Dave] Yeah!

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:31.120
- But the timing is very interesting.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:32.830
We had the Russia thing

00:35:32.830 --> 00:35:37.593
then we had the Ukraine impeachment thing.

00:35:37.593 --> 00:35:38.426
- Yeah, yeah.
- We have

00:35:38.426 --> 00:35:39.900
the daily fanaticism that apparently,

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:44.270
the tireless fanaticism, I
don't know what else to call it,

00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:47.906
to every single day run 18 articles

00:35:47.906 --> 00:35:51.410
accusing Trump of ghastly pathology

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:55.670
and anybody who supports
him as being just vermin

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:58.220
who needs to be exterminated
and that's been the ethos

00:35:58.220 --> 00:36:01.280
for "The New York Times"
with very few exceptions,

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:03.790
there are a few exceptions
but that's been the ethos,

00:36:03.790 --> 00:36:07.030
so I wonder sometimes
too if that explains,

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:08.820
maybe that doesn't explain
everything that's happened to us

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:11.447
but it explains this strange lust--

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.359
- Yeah, a lot, yeah.
- That we had

00:36:14.359 --> 00:36:15.619
in the early days, yeah.

00:36:15.619 --> 00:36:20.170
This burning passion,
like irrational desire

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:24.110
to bring the economy down,
to accuse Trump of something,

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:27.160
to, you know like in Batman,

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:28.910
'some people just wanna
see the world burn",

00:36:28.910 --> 00:36:30.169
you know that line?

00:36:30.169 --> 00:36:31.870
- I love that line from "The Joker",

00:36:31.870 --> 00:36:33.515
that's Heath Ledger's Joker, yeah.

00:36:33.515 --> 00:36:37.170
- Yeah, yeah, so look
I've thought of this too

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:40.240
and it's a extremely disturbing thought

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:43.960
because well let's just
say, if they imagine

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:44.930
that this was just gonna be

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:47.940
a political thing to take down Trump.

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:50.640
What they've done is they've
dismantled civilization,

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:53.110
however temporarily,
itself and I don't think

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:54.867
anybody could have imagined this

00:36:54.867 --> 00:36:58.400
and I've not talked to
Michael about this since then,

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:01.034
I hope, I would love to meet up with him

00:37:01.034 --> 00:37:03.540
because I think he's a
dear man and very charming

00:37:04.550 --> 00:37:06.850
but I wonder if even he is startled

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:08.850
about what he's unleashed and by the way,

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.270
I'm not sure that Michael
himself is responsible

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.540
for who his guests are,
you know what I mean?

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:15.500
You pick your guests

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:17.400
but you have full control of the program,

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:19.180
Michael's just the host of the program,

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:22.420
so I'm not sure Michael
bares any moral culpability

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:23.970
for what happened at "The New York Times"

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:25.440
but I wonder what he even think--

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:30.070
- Right, so I don't listen to the podcast

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:33.590
but it's sort of like,
if you think about it,

00:37:33.590 --> 00:37:36.770
it's a "New York Times"
podcast, we hate Trump,

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:39.030
we've tried to get Trump every other way,

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:41.050
why not bring on someone
that, as you said,

00:37:41.050 --> 00:37:43.270
is not a medical expert or anything else,

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:45.920
but a rhetorical language expert.

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:48.230
Let's make everyone
crazy because that fits

00:37:48.230 --> 00:37:49.063
everything else we're doing,

00:37:49.063 --> 00:37:51.040
but I wanted to jump to
something that you said before,

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:52.307
'cause I think this is
very interesting, you said,

00:37:52.307 --> 00:37:54.640
"I don't like dealing in counterfactuals,"

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:58.260
and I think one part of this
is how hindsight is 20/20

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:00.987
and everyone pretends
to be an expert today,

00:38:00.987 --> 00:38:04.270
when they were ignoring
things way back when,

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:08.250
so I checked and there was
no mention of coronavirus

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.650
at any of the Democratic debates

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:12.770
until the last one that they had

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:16.170
which was only Bernie and
Biden alone on March 25th

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:17.780
and that's when coronavirus was brought up

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:19.470
and there was no audience
and everything else.

00:38:19.470 --> 00:38:22.340
They had debates on February 7th,

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:25.520
February 19th and February 25th.

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:27.240
Now we've got the media running around

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:29.850
and all the Democratic
politicians Pelosi and Schumer

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:31.487
and everybody running around saying,

00:38:31.487 --> 00:38:35.247
"Trump should'a done something
in January, we all knew it,

00:38:35.247 --> 00:38:37.140
"blah, blah, blah", it
not only didn't come up

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.110
in any of their debates,

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:41.930
but he did try for a China
travel ban in January.

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:45.000
What do you make of that part of this?

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.990
That we just sort of look
at the people in charge

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:52.790
and we pick randomly who and
when they should've done this

00:38:52.790 --> 00:38:55.830
or that knowing that nobody
else did anything either.

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:57.680
It didn't come up once,

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:01.460
why didn't it come up in
the debate on February 19th?

00:39:02.551 --> 00:39:06.010
The one right before
the February 25th one,

00:39:06.010 --> 00:39:07.120
if everyone was talking about it

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:10.737
you'd think six days before,
nothing, strange huh?

00:39:11.665 --> 00:39:14.850
- Yeah but the looks of that
as a great scoop actually Dave,

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:16.330
I'd never even thought of that before.

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:20.997
I wrote my first article on
this topic, January 27th,

00:39:20.997 --> 00:39:23.910
I could have written about anything else

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:26.410
but I was watching what
was going on in China,

00:39:26.410 --> 00:39:28.160
I knew that quarantines might be coming,

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:29.350
so I decided to lean in

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:31.630
and I discussed this in great detail.

00:39:31.630 --> 00:39:32.470
Actually in January 27th,

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:35.430
the idea of this wouldn't
even be brought up,

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.790
two weeks later, two
and a half weeks later,

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:40.160
three weeks later and
the Democratic debate

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:43.222
is actually, that's extremely telling

00:39:43.222 --> 00:39:45.072
and yet everybody looks
back at Trump and says,

00:39:45.072 --> 00:39:46.297
"oh look, you're an idiot,

00:39:46.297 --> 00:39:48.950
"you didn't prepare
properly for this," so yeah,

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:50.902
so they had this hindsight thing,

00:39:50.902 --> 00:39:53.101
which is fine, we're
not omniscient figures.

00:39:53.101 --> 00:39:56.780
We don't know what's going-
we're all, Ross Douthat

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:58.960
about this beautiful article
again in "The New York Times"

00:39:58.960 --> 00:40:00.880
today, so they run good stuff.

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:05.880
Beautiful article about
how few people there are

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:09.634
are experts about this at
all but you would think

00:40:09.634 --> 00:40:12.350
that early on, that they
would have consulted-

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:14.160
that's what bothers me most about this

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:16.310
and then it just drives
me crazy, actually,

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:22.240
that once they realize the
coronavirus was a thing,

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:26.820
why weren't they calling
epidemiologists and experts

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:29.640
and medical professionals and
people with specializations

00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:32.936
in dealing pandemic diseases
and asking what they think?

00:40:32.936 --> 00:40:36.220
Why weren't they calling John Honiotes

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:41.220
or Knut Wittkowski from
Rockefeller University

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:45.130
or why didn't they pay any attention

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:49.155
to the 800 medical
professionals, epidemiologists

00:40:49.155 --> 00:40:52.634
and biostatisticians that signed a letter

00:40:52.634 --> 00:40:57.440
put up by Yale University
begging our nations leaders

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:00.690
and political people to
not shut down the economy,

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:04.100
to not pursue this locked-in strategy.

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:07.950
Why wasn't the press
even slightly interested

00:41:07.950 --> 00:41:11.490
in the fact that 800 medical professions

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:13.660
associated with Yale University

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:17.820
wrote a public letter begging
America's political leaders

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:20.560
to not do exactly what they did.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:22.670
Where has been the press throughout this?

00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:27.010
Why has it been you, "Reason" magazine,

00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:29.150
American Institute for Economic Research

00:41:29.150 --> 00:41:31.540
and just a handful of other venues

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:33.090
that have been left to report

00:41:33.965 --> 00:41:38.660
what medical professionals
are saying, can you imagine?

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.750
I think about a guy like
Knut Wittkowski okay,

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:49.500
so it's like 35 years
of researching viruses,

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:51.810
their transmission, the
policy response to them,

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:54.220
how they work, what you should do.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:56.770
This is a serious guy, one of the heads

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:59.020
of the Human Genome Project,

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.900
this is his life and he lives in New York

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:05.840
and this whole world
unfolds before his eyes

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:07.880
and that whole world's
collapsing around him.

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:09.530
His phone didn't ring,

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:13.090
he didn't get a single
email, nobody asked him

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:17.510
and finally you're just desperate
and shocked and horrified.

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:20.676
How do you spend your life as
a professional in this area

00:42:20.676 --> 00:42:23.790
and then when the critical
moment in your life comes along

00:42:23.790 --> 00:42:26.867
where civilization could
use your expertise,

00:42:26.867 --> 00:42:29.080
everyone completely ignores you

00:42:29.080 --> 00:42:30.920
and you're just some dude siting alone

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:32.850
and you have to wait for
an independent filmmaker

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:34.490
to come along and do
an interview with you.

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:35.930
Same thing happened John Honiotes

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:39.630
who had an article in
2005 about fake science

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:43.130
that took over the world,
he was like a media star.

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.640
He's exposed fake science his whole life,

00:42:45.640 --> 00:42:47.820
he's a specialist in biostatistics

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:49.830
and he had a lot to say about this stuff.

00:42:49.830 --> 00:42:53.730
Nobody asked him, we destroyed the world

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:56.660
without ever consulting the
actual medical professionals

00:42:56.660 --> 00:43:00.760
about this, not "The New
York Times", nobody cared

00:43:00.760 --> 00:43:04.050
about the actual
specialists in these fields

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:05.500
who were pleading and begging

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:08.310
and trying to find somebody to talk to

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:10.640
and get their message out to
the point that Yale University

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:13.200
actually had to distribute
this open letter

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:14.760
from 800 medical professionals

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:17.230
which was subsequently completely ignored

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:18.210
by the mainstream press.

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:23.210
It's a disgrace, we're gonna
be dealing with this for years

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:26.080
and I think that there's
gonna be hell to pay

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:29.170
on the part of the mainstream
press for what they've done,

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:30.500
what they've done to the world,

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:34.677
the missed opportunities,
the morally egregious abuse

00:43:34.677 --> 00:43:37.920
of their power over the
public mind in the last month,

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:42.530
it sickens me more than anything
else I've seen in my life

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:47.004
and I like a free press, I
believe in press freedom,

00:43:47.004 --> 00:43:48.760
I believe in all these things.

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:53.553
I think it can and does
work but this is an abuse,

00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:59.340
to cheer on the end of our
rights and liberties like this

00:43:59.340 --> 00:44:02.200
and so cavalierly and if you're right,

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:04.690
as you are just asking the questions

00:44:04.690 --> 00:44:06.170
and I'm asking the questions too,

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:10.100
was this all driven by a
pathological hatred of Trump?

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:12.730
Is this it, is that what drove it all?

00:44:12.730 --> 00:44:14.859
I don't know but if that's true,

00:44:14.859 --> 00:44:17.853
somebody needs to be held accountable.

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:20.830
- You know it's so
interesting listening to you

00:44:20.830 --> 00:44:23.160
because there is this sort of undercurrent

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:28.160
of you're a non-alarmist
when it comes to COVID itself

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:30.550
and you're trying to figure
out how we can be free

00:44:30.550 --> 00:44:32.510
and actually, in the last five minutes

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:37.510
what really animates your
fear factor, your fear senses

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:40.610
is everything else related to it

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:43.800
and I think that is where
a lot of people are at

00:44:43.800 --> 00:44:48.280
where we're at least
confused as to the real depth

00:44:48.280 --> 00:44:51.420
of the fear we should have
related to the virus itself

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:54.280
and most people are trying their best

00:44:54.280 --> 00:44:56.240
to do the best they can and not go outside

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:59.280
and the rest of it but that's
not really where the fear is.

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:01.110
I actually think that the fear

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:02.680
is what you're talking about right now,

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:06.830
that it seems like every
sense-making machine

00:45:06.830 --> 00:45:09.320
that we've had is no longer functioning

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:12.330
or is trying to manipulate
us, we're seeing through it

00:45:12.330 --> 00:45:16.050
in a very clear way and
that is freaking scary

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:19.890
and especially for people
who maybe not my audience,

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:21.770
that's seen this for a while

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:23.420
or the younger internet audience

00:45:23.420 --> 00:45:25.110
but if you're a boomer,

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:27.360
not to pile on the boomers all the time

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.600
but if you're the type of person

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:30.770
that really isn't that
checked-in to this stuff

00:45:30.770 --> 00:45:32.820
but now you're really starting to see it

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:35.120
and you're seeing that
the future of your life,

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:40.010
your retirement, savings,
all of these things

00:45:40.010 --> 00:45:41.900
are gonna change drastically.

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:45.270
The world that you thought
existed doesn't exist anymore,

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:47.462
that's, I think, much scarier

00:45:47.462 --> 00:45:51.110
than actually getting sick at the moment.

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:51.943
That's a real flip

00:45:51.943 --> 00:45:54.250
that you wouldn't hear
on mainstream media.

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:59.040
- No but you're exactly right
and I knew that this was true

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:02.400
from, I would say probably,
the first week of March

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:05.610
that the most terrifying
thing was not the virus.

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:10.610
You know we deal with annual
new flu strains every year

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:14.025
and we have for 150,000 years actually.

00:46:14.025 --> 00:46:16.400
We have medical professionals
who deal with this.

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:20.260
We have therapies, we
have ways to deal with it

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:23.060
and then we have tests, we have vaccines,

00:46:23.060 --> 00:46:25.900
we have herd immunity strategies,

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:28.780
we know what we're doing
in dealing with disease

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:32.240
but when it comes to
dealing with a totalitarian

00:46:34.340 --> 00:46:38.470
state apparatus that's taken
away your rights and liberties

00:46:38.470 --> 00:46:43.470
as cheered on by a howling,
deeply ignorant media apparatus,

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:46.350
that's what's really scary.

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:48.370
I was writing this morning, Dave,

00:46:48.370 --> 00:46:50.840
if I could just quickly
tell you about this

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:51.920
and I'm sure we all have our own stories.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:52.795
- [Dave] Yeah please.

00:46:52.795 --> 00:46:57.000
- But I have an accountant here

00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:59.270
at the American Institute
for Economic Research

00:46:59.270 --> 00:47:02.240
who's hounding me for
my receipts from March

00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:07.240
and I'm busy, I couldn't get
to it until this morning, busy

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:08.730
but then you know what it's like

00:47:08.730 --> 00:47:10.350
to go through your receipts,
you've been through that,

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:11.183
you've been there before,

00:47:11.183 --> 00:47:13.860
so now the receipts,
Dave, from March first,

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:16.490
so I got to see every Amtrak I took,

00:47:16.490 --> 00:47:19.863
every restaurant I ate at,
the hotels I stayed at,

00:47:19.863 --> 00:47:24.863
the taxi cabs I took, various things,

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:27.960
glassware I bought for
the house and so on,

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:31.410
so I had to relive this
month through my receipts

00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:35.060
and it was horrifying,
I'd really had a sense

00:47:35.060 --> 00:47:37.330
that this is the old world
and this is the new world

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:40.943
but one day I seized on was March 12th

00:47:40.943 --> 00:47:45.010
because I was in the city
going to a television interview

00:47:45.010 --> 00:47:47.960
and I was there with
my friend Taleed Brown

00:47:47.960 --> 00:47:52.960
and the city was starting to
panic, it was very interesting

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:55.880
and we got there about
10:30 and my interview

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:58.230
wasn't really 'till four so I
had about four hours to kill,

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:01.800
so I went to my favorite
Irish pub and we sat down

00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:03.780
and there were, instead of open tables,

00:48:03.780 --> 00:48:05.740
there was suddenly paper
on the tablecloths.

00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:09.340
This is a time when people
knew that something was coming

00:48:10.284 --> 00:48:12.090
but they didn't know what it was.

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:14.802
They knew for sure by this
point that there was a virus

00:48:14.802 --> 00:48:17.440
and probably it was in New York

00:48:17.440 --> 00:48:22.260
but that's not what was really in the air.

00:48:22.260 --> 00:48:25.913
What was in the air was
the fear of the response.

00:48:27.693 --> 00:48:30.870
We can deal with viruses,
don't sneeze on me,

00:48:30.870 --> 00:48:33.820
don't cough on me, maybe
set my phone down over there

00:48:33.820 --> 00:48:35.470
I'll pick it up, we can deal with that.

00:48:35.470 --> 00:48:36.920
Okay, I caught the virus,

00:48:36.920 --> 00:48:38.900
gosh I'm gonna be down a
couple of days and so on,

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:41.690
we can deal with that but the idea

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:43.763
of suddenly having the Amtrak close?

00:48:45.400 --> 00:48:46.500
We didn't know what was gonna happen

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:49.680
if the streets shut down, the
cops arresting people in bars,

00:48:49.680 --> 00:48:51.667
we didn't know what was gonna happen

00:48:51.667 --> 00:48:55.500
and Taleed and I sat there
between 10:30 and 11,

00:48:55.500 --> 00:48:57.470
I tell you Dave, what was
very interesting that,

00:48:57.470 --> 00:49:00.920
I have to write about
this, but bars were packed.

00:49:00.920 --> 00:49:04.210
They were packed not because
people thought life was normal

00:49:04.210 --> 00:49:06.580
but they just were certain
that life was about

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:10.670
to become really unnormal
and they were drinking.

00:49:10.670 --> 00:49:11.610
They were just gathered around

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:13.090
and drinking themselves silly.

00:49:13.090 --> 00:49:17.777
I said to the person who's
waiting on us, I said,

00:49:17.777 --> 00:49:20.590
"have you ever seen this
kind of drinking at 11AM?"

00:49:20.590 --> 00:49:22.137
She goes, "I've been here five years,

00:49:22.137 --> 00:49:25.147
"I've never seen anything like this",

00:49:25.147 --> 00:49:28.180
people just drinking
themselves silly out of fear

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:30.159
not of the virus so much.

00:49:30.159 --> 00:49:33.810
- Yeah, of the other thing.
- But of the public panic

00:49:33.810 --> 00:49:37.741
and the political response
and those hours that went by

00:49:37.741 --> 00:49:41.650
between 11 and four when I
finally did my interview,

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:43.630
were terrifying 'cause I just didn't know

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:45.850
if we could even get the next train out

00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:49.740
and after the interview,
we rushed to Penn Station

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:52.750
just as fast as we could and
when we got on that Amtrak,

00:49:52.750 --> 00:49:54.800
we were both just
breathing a sigh of relief

00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:58.230
and even then I wasn't sure
if the train was gonna move,

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:01.330
so when it started moving
and I looked at Taleed

00:50:01.330 --> 00:50:04.287
and I said, "I know it's a two hour trip

00:50:04.287 --> 00:50:06.602
"but when that train stops at Hudson,

00:50:06.602 --> 00:50:08.831
"that's when my heart
is gonna stop racing,

00:50:08.831 --> 00:50:12.250
"'cause this is terrifying," yeah.

00:50:12.250 --> 00:50:14.870
- Yeah and there's so
many versions of this.

00:50:14.870 --> 00:50:16.720
One of the things that I've
been thinking about personally

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:19.030
is my grandmother-in-law is 85,

00:50:19.030 --> 00:50:21.050
lives alone in New Jersey,
none of her children

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:22.980
or grandchildren live in New Jersey,

00:50:22.980 --> 00:50:24.690
so we've been having food delivered to her

00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:26.610
and I have a friend that
lives about 30 miles away

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:29.549
who's dropped off some stuff but the idea

00:50:29.549 --> 00:50:33.310
that what if the states close
their borders, you know?

00:50:33.310 --> 00:50:36.558
What if you can't get food to somebody?

00:50:36.558 --> 00:50:38.750
All of these things that
we thought these things

00:50:38.750 --> 00:50:40.950
would never be possible
in an American contest,

00:50:40.950 --> 00:50:42.960
the fact that all of us right now

00:50:42.960 --> 00:50:44.960
are eating home all the time

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:48.500
and just hoping that Amazon
can keep delivering things.

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:50.980
I actually, it's a weird thing

00:50:50.980 --> 00:50:52.100
because the silver lining is,

00:50:52.100 --> 00:50:54.480
I think maybe we did need
a shock to our system

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:57.500
to understand really what is freedom

00:50:57.500 --> 00:51:00.710
and what is being independent

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:02.330
and not so reliant on the system,

00:51:02.330 --> 00:51:04.830
so I am enthused about that

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:06.500
but I'm also worried that a lot of people

00:51:06.500 --> 00:51:08.510
haven't ever thought about it before

00:51:08.510 --> 00:51:10.564
and that now they're gonna
suddenly think about it

00:51:10.564 --> 00:51:13.850
and then who knows which way they'll go

00:51:13.850 --> 00:51:15.387
because they might just
put their hands up and say,

00:51:15.387 --> 00:51:18.437
"no, just help me government,
please do everything for me,

00:51:18.437 --> 00:51:21.110
"I'm just completely
incapable of doing anything."

00:51:21.110 --> 00:51:22.984
- I wonder what the
response is going to be

00:51:22.984 --> 00:51:26.027
and I think that's partly
your job and partly at my job

00:51:26.027 --> 00:51:30.230
in the days and weeks and months coming,

00:51:30.230 --> 00:51:33.560
if ideas matter at all, getting
out the message of freedom

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:37.090
and liberty and prosperity and
safety through those things

00:51:37.090 --> 00:51:41.117
is, I can't imagine more important
work than that right now.

00:51:41.117 --> 00:51:44.301
It's going to require voices like yours

00:51:44.301 --> 00:51:48.600
